/* data/learn-c/chem-12-05.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/chem-12-05.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-05"] = {"5.1":{"s":"Electrochemistry deals with turning chemical energy into electrical energy and back. A dry cell makes electricity from a redox reaction; electrolysis uses electricity to drive a reaction that would not happen by itself. The chapter also covers how well solutions of electrolytes conduct, and how cells, batteries and fuel cells work."},"5.2":{"s":"Metals conduct by the flow of electrons, with no chemical change. Electrolytes conduct by the movement of ions, in solution or when molten, and this carries matter along with a chemical change at the electrodes. Substances like sugar and urea do not ionise in water and are non-electrolytes. Strong electrolytes (salts, strong acids and bases) ionise almost fully; weak ones (acetic acid, ammonia) only slightly.","w":["Ionic solids do not conduct; they conduct only when molten or dissolved, when the ions can move."]},"5.3":{"s":"Conductance G is 1/R. Conductivity k is the conductance of a 1 cm cube of solution. Molar conductivity Λ is the conductance of all the ions from one mole of electrolyte. On dilution k falls (fewer ions per cm³), but Λ rises. For strong electrolytes Λ rises slowly and linearly with √c; for weak ones it rises sharply. Kohlrausch's law gives Λ at zero concentration as the sum of the ion contributions, even for weak electrolytes.","f":[{"x":"G = 1/R","n":"Conductance; unit siemens, S = ohm^-1.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.3, p.92"},{"x":"k = (1/R)*(l/a)","n":"Conductivity; l/a = cell constant (distance between electrodes / electrode area).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.3, p.92"},{"x":"Lambda = 1000*k/c","n":"Molar conductivity in ohm^-1 cm^2 mol^-1 when k is in ohm^-1 cm^-1 and c in mol/L.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.3, p.93"},{"x":"Lambda = Lambda_0 - a*sqrt(c)","n":"Strong electrolytes: linear in the square root of concentration; Lambda_0 at zero concentration.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.3, p.94"},{"x":"Lambda_0 = n_(+)*lambda_(0+) + n_(-)*lambda_(0-)","n":"Kohlrausch's law; n = number of each ion in the formula.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.3, p.94"},{"x":"alpha = Lambda_c/Lambda_0","n":"Degree of dissociation of a weak electrolyte.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.3, p.95"}],"w":["Dilution: conductivity k DECREASES, molar conductivity Λ INCREASES.","Λ₀ of a weak electrolyte cannot be read from the graph; get it from Kohlrausch's law using strong electrolytes."]},"5.4":{"s":"An electrochemical cell has two electrodes in an electrolyte. Oxidation happens at the anode and reduction at the cathode. In an electrolytic cell an outside DC source forces a non-spontaneous reaction: electrical energy becomes chemical energy, and the anode is positive. In a galvanic (voltaic) cell a spontaneous reaction makes electricity, and the anode is negative.","w":["Anode is always where oxidation happens; only its SIGN changes: + in electrolytic, − in galvanic cells."]},"5.5":{"s":"In electrolysis of molten NaCl, sodium metal forms at the cathode and chlorine gas at the anode. In aqueous NaCl, water is reduced at the cathode instead, giving hydrogen and OH⁻ (so NaOH forms), while chlorine is still given off at the anode. The amount of product depends on the charge passed: one faraday (96500 C) is the charge on one mole of electrons.","f":[{"x":"Q = I*t","n":"Charge in coulombs; I in amperes, t in seconds.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.5, p.100"},{"x":"moles of electrons = Q/96500","n":"One faraday = 96500 C per mole of electrons.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.5, p.100"},{"x":"W = (I*t/96500)*(mole ratio)*(molar mass)","n":"Mass of product at an electrode; mole ratio from the half reaction (e.g. 1/2 for Cu2+ to Cu).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.5, p.100"},{"x":"W_1/((mole ratio)_1*M_1) = W_2/((mole ratio)_2*M_2)","n":"Two cells in series pass the same charge.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.5, p.101"}],"w":["Convert minutes to seconds before using Q = I t.","Aqueous NaCl gives H₂ at the cathode, not sodium: water is easier to reduce than Na⁺."]},"5.6":{"s":"A galvanic cell has two half cells, each a metal dipping into a solution of its own ions, joined by a wire and a salt bridge. The salt bridge (KCl or NH₄NO₃ in agar) completes the circuit, stops the solutions mixing and keeps each one electrically neutral. In cell notation the anode is written on the left, the cathode on the right, a single line marks a phase boundary and a double line the salt bridge.","w":["Cell notation: anode | anode ions || cathode ions | cathode. Left is oxidation, right is reduction."]},"5.7":{"s":"The potential difference between an electrode and its solution is the electrode potential. By convention the standard potential is quoted as a standard reduction potential (1 M, 1 atm, 25 °C). The cell potential is the cathode's reduction potential minus the anode's. The Nernst equation gives how the cell or electrode potential changes when concentrations are not standard.","f":[{"x":"E^0_cell = E^0(cathode) - E^0(anode)","n":"Standard cell potential from standard reduction potentials.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.7, p.105"},{"x":"E_cell = E^0_cell - (2.303*R*T/(n*F))*log_10([C]^c*[D]^d/([A]^a*[B]^b))","n":"Nernst equation for aA + bB -> cC + dD; n = moles of electrons.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.7, p.105"},{"x":"E_cell = E^0_cell - (0.0592/n)*log_10(Q)","n":"Nernst equation at 25 degrees C; Q = the concentration ratio above.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.7, p.105"}],"w":["Pure solids and liquids are left out of Q (taken as 1); gases enter as partial pressures.","Do not multiply E⁰ when you multiply a half reaction to balance electrons: E⁰ is intensive."]},"5.8":{"s":"The electrical work a cell can do equals the fall in its Gibbs energy. A reaction is spontaneous when ΔG is negative, which means the cell potential is positive. The standard cell potential is linked to the equilibrium constant of the cell reaction, so a cell measurement gives K. Doubling the reaction doubles ΔG⁰ but not E⁰, which is an intensive property.","f":[{"x":"Delta G = -n*F*E_cell","n":"Gibbs energy change of the cell reaction; F = 96500 C/mol.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.8, p.107"},{"x":"Delta G^0 = -R*T*ln(K)","n":"Standard Gibbs energy and equilibrium constant.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.8, p.107"},{"x":"E^0_cell = (0.0592/n)*log_10(K)","n":"Standard cell potential and equilibrium constant at 25 degrees C.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.8, p.107"}],"w":["E positive ⇔ ΔG negative ⇔ spontaneous; K > 1 when E⁰ is positive."]},"5.9":{"s":"Only the potential of a whole cell can be measured, so one electrode is chosen as a reference with potential zero: the standard hydrogen electrode, a platinised platinum plate in 1 M H⁺ with hydrogen gas at 1 atm bubbling over it. Joined to another electrode, the measured cell voltage gives that electrode's standard potential. The SHE is hard to use in practice, so secondary references such as the calomel electrode are common.","f":[{"x":"E^0_cell = E^0_H2 - E^0_Zn = -E^0_Zn","n":"SHE as cathode with zinc as anode gives the zinc electrode's standard potential.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.9, p.109"}],"w":["The SHE's 0.000 V is a choice (a convention), not a measured value."]},"5.10":{"s":"Primary cells cannot be recharged: the reaction stops once the chemicals are used up, as in the dry (Leclanché) cell. (The book lists the mercury cell as secondary.) Secondary cells can be recharged by passing current backwards, as in the lead storage battery (spongy lead anode, PbO₂ cathode, 38% sulphuric acid) and the nickel-cadmium cell. In the lead battery both plates turn to PbSO₄ on discharge and the acid gets weaker.","w":["Lead battery: the density of the acid falls during discharge, so a hydrometer shows the state of charge."]},"5.11":{"s":"A fuel cell turns the energy of a combustion reaction directly into electricity, with the fuel and oxidant fed in continuously. In the hydrogen-oxygen cell, hydrogen is oxidised at a porous carbon anode and oxygen reduced at the cathode in hot aqueous KOH; the only product is water. Fuel cells are about 70% efficient and non-polluting, but hydrogen is costly and hazardous to handle. They have powered spacecraft.","f":[{"x":"2H_2 + O_2 -> 2H_2O","n":"Net reaction of the hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell; E^0_cell = 1.23 V.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 5.11, p.113"}],"w":["Hydrogen and oxygen never react directly in the cell; electrons pass through the outside circuit."]},"5.12":{"s":"The electrochemical series lists half reactions as reductions in order of standard potential. A higher (more positive) E⁰ means a stronger oxidising agent on the left side; a lower (more negative) E⁰ means a stronger reducing agent on the right side. A species can oxidise any reducing agent below it. A cell reaction is spontaneous when the species with the higher E⁰ is reduced, giving a positive cell potential.","w":["Strongest oxidising agent: top left (F₂). Strongest reducing agent: bottom right (Li)."]}};
